content.paragraph:
where $C$ is the consumption matrix $\begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{c}_{1} & \textbf{c}_{2} & \textbf{c}_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}$, namely

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      \textbf{x} & {= \textbf{d} + C\textbf{d} + C^{2}\textbf{d} + C^{3}\textbf{d} + \cdots} \\
       & {= (I + C + C^{2} + C^{3} + \cdots)\textbf{d}}
      \end{matrix}$$

$$\boxed{\begin{array}{l}
      {(I - C)^{- 1} \approx I + C + C^{2} + C^{3} + \cdots + C^{m}} \\
      {\text{when~the~column~sums~of~}C\text{~are~less~than~1.}}
      \end{array}}$$

statement.paragraph:
Consider the production model $\textbf{x} = C\textbf{x} + \textbf{d}$ for an economy with two sectors, where$$\begin{array}{lll}
      {C = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      .0 & .5 \\
      .6 & .2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,} & & {\mathbf{d} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      50 \\
      30
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{array}$$
